LIVRO = 'Veganismo Baseado em Evidências (Evidence-Based Veganism, in Portuguese)'
INCERTEZA = '''<div class="bloco curta"><p><strong>How to decide under uncertainty.</strong> Instead of asking only "is this being sentient, yes or no?", ask how likely it is and weigh that by the size of the harm or benefit at stake. A moderate probability multiplied by an immense scale can outweigh a high certainty over small numbers. Uncertainty about a borderline case does not contaminate the certainty about the criterion: sentience is still what matters.</p></div>'''

ALEM = {
 'antiespecismo': dict(s='antispeciesism', selo='Foundation', t='Antispeciesism and sentientism',
  res='Veganism is one consequence of a broader criterion: what matters morally is the capacity to be harmed or benefited.',
  corpo='''<h2>Two names, one idea</h2><p><strong>Sentientism</strong> holds that what matters morally is the capacity to be harmed or benefited, present in any sentient being, human or not. <strong>Antispeciesism</strong> is the consequence: giving similar weight to similar harms, whatever the species.</p><p>That is why the discussion does not end at the plate. If we accept the criterion, it also applies to what we do by omission, to animals no one exploits and to beings that may come to exist.</p>
<h2>Sentientism: what it is and what it is not</h2>
<p><strong>Sentientism</strong> (also called <strong>sentiocentrism</strong>) is the position that what matters morally is the capacity to have positive and negative experiences. A being that can be harmed or benefited from the point of view of the one living the situation has interests of its own, and those interests count in themselves, not because of what others do with the being. Jeremy Bentham's question, "can they suffer?", is usually cited as the origin of the idea (<a href="https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-animal/">Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy</a>). The term was already in use in 1977, in a text by John Rodman (<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sentientism">Wikipedia</a>).</p>
<p><strong>Sentience is not only pain.</strong> It covers anything that can be lived as good or bad: pain, fear, frustration, boredom, comfort, pleasure, play, attachment. So the criterion includes positive well-being, not only the absence of suffering. The scientific declarations cited below speak of consciousness, and here are read as evidence about sentience.</p>
<h3>Criterion, consequence and practice</h3>
<ul><li><strong>Sentientism</strong> is the criterion: who is inside the moral circle.</li><li><strong>Antispeciesism</strong> is a consequence: if what counts is sentience, species cannot be the factor that decides whether an interest weighs or not.</li><li><strong>Veganism</strong> is a practice that follows for the use of animals. Sentientism goes further: it also applies to animals no one exploits, to invertebrates and, if it comes to that, to digital minds.</li></ul>
<p>The criterion is defended, with important differences among them, by <strong>Peter Singer</strong> (for whom the capacity to suffer is a prerequisite for having interests), <strong>Oscar Horta</strong> and <strong>Luciano Carlos Cunha</strong> (who take it to wild animal suffering, and who work in Brazil through the organization Animal Ethics), and the <strong>Sentientism</strong> movement, developed by <strong>Jamie Woodhouse</strong>, which describes it as a worldview built on evidence, reason and compassion for all sentient beings (<a href="https://sentientism.info/">sentientism.info</a>). <strong>Jeff Sebo</strong> argues for widening the moral circle to beings with a non-negligible chance of well-being, including invertebrates and AI, but he combines sentience with other possible bases, such as agency. <strong>Jonathan Birch</strong> works on the practical problem of acting without knowing who feels (see below). Not every defender accepts every conclusion of the others.</p>

<h2>The variants of sentientism</h2>
<p>Accepting that sentience matters leaves two questions open. The criteria table on the site <em>Ética Para Iniciantes</em> (Ethics for Beginners, in Portuguese) calls the answers partial versions of sentientism, as opposed to the full version.</p>
<h3>Gradualist or non-gradualist</h3>
<p>The question: <strong>does an experience of the same intensity count equally in any being?</strong></p>
<ul><li>The <strong>non-gradualist</strong> says yes: being sentient is enough, and an equal pain weighs the same, whether in a human or a pig.</li><li>The <strong>gradualist</strong> says the weight varies with capacities such as intelligence and self-awareness: more complex minds would have interests of greater weight. There is also a reading in which the weight varies with the degree or richness of sentience itself.</li></ul>
<p>One distinction keeps the positions from being confused. <strong>What is at stake</strong> changes with capacities: a being with plans and an anticipated future loses more by dying than one without, and almost no one disputes that. <strong>The weight of an equal harm</strong> is the gradualism question proper.</p>
<p><strong>Who holds what.</strong> Singer defends the principle of equal consideration of interests, which is not equal treatment nor equal weight for everything (see <a href="../argumentos/n14.html">N14</a>). Shelly Kagan, in <em>How to Count Animals, More or Less</em> (2019), defends a hierarchy: persons count more than animals and some animals count more than others. David DeGrazia criticized Kagan's claim that humans lacking the capacities of persons get extra status.</p>
<p><strong>Main objections.</strong> (1) The argument from marginal cases: if intelligence sets the weight, infants and people with severe cognitive disability would weigh less, and many adult animals would weigh more than they do. Gradualist replies appeal to potential, to relationships or to Kagan's "modal personhood", and each is contested. (2) The intensity of pain may not track intelligence: a simple mind, with no consolation or distraction, may suffer a great deal.</p>
<h3>Temporalist or non-temporalist</h3>
<p>The question: <strong>does suffering or well-being count for less because it happens later?</strong></p>
<ul><li>The <strong>temporalist</strong> gives less weight to the future, because of uncertainty, distance, or the view that future beings are less real, applying something like a discount rate.</li><li>The <strong>non-temporalist</strong> (atemporalist) says the date does not change the value: like distance in space, it changes what we know, not what it is worth.</li></ul>
<p>Derek Parfit distinguished discounting for <strong>uncertainty</strong>, which is legitimate (we weigh by probability), from <strong>pure</strong> discounting, merely for being later, which he rejected; Henry Sidgwick had already said that time does not change value. Longtermism (Toby Ord, William MacAskill) takes the distant future seriously, with criticisms about fanaticism. Objection: today's decisions change <em>who</em> will exist (Parfit's non-identity problem), which complicates talk of "harming" future beings.</p>
<h3>The four combinations</h3>
<p>The table below is a reading of the logical implications of each combination, not a classification published by any author.</p>
<div class="tabela"><table><tr><th scope="col">Combination</th><th scope="col">Insects</th><th scope="col">Fetuses</th><th scope="col">Future beings</th><th scope="col">Death</th></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Non-gradualist and non-temporalist</strong> (full version)</td><td>Count if sentient; an equal experience weighs the same</td><td>Count from the moment there is sentience, an open empirical question</td><td>Count like present beings, discounting only for uncertainty</td><td>Matters for what the being loses, not for its species</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Gradualist and non-temporalist</strong></td><td>Count with a lower individual weight; the huge numbers may compensate (<a href="../argumentos/n14.html">N14</a>)</td><td>Lower weight while capacities are lower</td><td>Count like present beings, weighted by capacities</td><td>Weighs more for more complex minds</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Non-gradualist and temporalist</strong></td><td>As in the full version</td><td>As in the full version</td><td>Count less the further away in time</td><td>Loss of future life weighs less the more distant it is</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Gradualist and temporalist</strong></td><td>Lower individual weight</td><td>Lower weight</td><td>Lower weight twice: by capacity and by distance</td><td>Weighs less, except for complex minds and near lives</td></tr></table></div>
<p>Two caveats. Whether a fetus feels, and when, depends on neuroscience, not on the variant; Birch discusses it among the cases of uncertain sentience. And on death there is a third question, that of the <strong>interest in living</strong>: Elizabeth Harman argues that painlessly killing a healthy animal is a significant harm, against the idea that only pain matters.</p>
<h2>Sentientism and the other criteria</h2>
<div class="tabela"><table><tr><th scope="col">Criterion</th><th scope="col">What counts</th><th scope="col">Who is left out</th><th scope="col">Main problem</th><th scope="col">Examples</th></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Anthropocentrism</strong></td><td>Humans</td><td>All nonhumans, or they get only indirect duties</td><td>Species is a biological trait; reason and language are missing in some humans who count and present in some animals who do not (marginal cases)</td><td>Kant, with indirect duties toward animals (SEP)</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Rationality, autonomy, personhood</strong></td><td>Those who reason and govern themselves</td><td>Infants, people with severe cognitive disability and most animals</td><td>Same marginal-cases difficulty; Tom Regan answers with "subject of a life", which asks for less than rationality</td><td>Kant; Regan</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Social relations and contract</strong></td><td>Those who take part in agreements, bonds or reciprocity</td><td>Those who cannot bargain, reciprocate or have a recognized bond</td><td>Turns bargaining power into value; Mark Rowlands uses the veil of ignorance to include animals</td><td>Contractualism; Rowlands</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Interest in living</strong></td><td>Beings with preferences about the future</td><td>Those who feel but do not plan</td><td>Explains why death matters, not why pain matters; usually added to sentientism, not opposed to it</td><td>Singer; Harman, in a different sense</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Biocentrism</strong></td><td>Every living being</td><td>Only what is not alive; among the living, no one</td><td>Having a good of its own without being able to feel creates no one who cares; biocentrists reply that having a good of its own is enough</td><td>Schweitzer, Taylor, Attfield</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Ecocentrism and holism</strong></td><td>Ecosystems, species, the biosphere</td><td>The individuals inside them, in a conflict</td><td>The whole does not feel; Regan called putting the whole first "environmental fascism"</td><td>Leopold, Callicott, Naess</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Sentientism</strong></td><td>Whoever can have good or bad experiences</td><td>Those who are not sentient (if any)</td><td>Depends on knowing who feels; comparing experiences across species is hard</td><td>Singer, Horta, Cunha, Woodhouse</td></tr></table></div>
<h2>Possibilities and limits</h2>
<h3>Who is sentient?</h3>
<p>We do not observe another being's experience, only signs in behavior, brain and evolution. Two declarations by researchers help, without closing the question. The <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cambridge_Declaration_on_Consciousness">Cambridge Declaration (2012)</a> states that humans are not unique in having the neurological substrates that generate consciousness, and mentions mammals, birds and octopuses. The <a href="https://sites.google.com/nyu.edu/nydeclaration/declaration">New York Declaration (2024)</a> says there is strong scientific support for mammals and birds and at least a realistic possibility of conscious experience in all vertebrates and many invertebrates (cephalopods, decapod crustaceans and insects), and that ignoring that possibility is irresponsible. Both are statements by experts, not proof.</p>
<p>Under uncertainty, Birch proposes treating as "sentience candidates" the systems with a credible, non-negligible possibility of feeling, and acting proportionately to the evidence, without demanding certainty (<a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Edge_of_Sentience"><em>The Edge of Sentience</em></a>, 2024). This is the benefit-of-the-doubt argument, valid in any variant.</p>
<h3>Moral weight</h3>
<p>Giving consideration to insects is not giving an ant the same weight as a person. Provisional estimates place insects well below mammals, but the number of individuals is huge. Chapter <a href="../argumentos/n14.html">N14</a> covers this.</p>
<h3>Wild animals</h3>
<p>If sentience is the criterion, suffering that no one caused also counts. Horta and Kyle Johannsen reach this by different routes, and the central point is to research before intervening. See <a href="animais-selvagens.html">wild animal suffering</a>.</p>
<h3>Digital minds</h3>
<p>The criterion does not depend on the material, so an artificial system that felt would count. Today none is recognized as sentient, and there is no consensus on how we would know. See <a href="ia-e-robos.html">sentience in AI and robots</a>.</p>
<h2>What is not speciesism</h2><p>Recognizing real differences is not discrimination. Saying an animal does not need to vote is an observation. Saying it can suffer without that mattering, because it is not very intelligent, is speciesism. The line between recognizing a difference and using it to discard interests is where the debate should happen.</p>
<h2>Hierarchies that survive among vegans</h2><p>People who have already rejected exploitation for food can keep speciesism in other forms:</p><ul><li><strong>Humans above animals</strong> in any conflict of interests, accepting experimentation or ritual sacrifice.</li><li><strong>Pets above farmed animals.</strong></li><li><strong>Mammals and birds above fish</strong>, who are numerically the largest victims of exploitation.</li><li><strong>Vertebrates above invertebrates</strong>, dismissing insects and decapods without examination.</li><li><strong>Exploited animals above wild ones</strong>, which reveals an omission bias: who caused the harm matters more than who suffers it.</li><li><strong>Abstract concepts above individuals</strong>, such as biodiversity or ecosystem balance.</li><li><strong>Those who exist now above those yet to come</strong>, a time discount that disregards future suffering.</li></ul>
<p>The test is the veil of ignorance: would you accept the hierarchy without knowing in which position you would be born? There are billions of humans and quintillions of animals, counting invertebrates. No one who took that ratio seriously would bet on a hierarchy with humans systematically on top.</p>
<h2>Humanism taken all the way</h2><p>This is not anti-humanism. Humanism brought universality and impartiality, which helped overcome hierarchies between human groups, but those principles stopped at the species line without justification. Posthumanism keeps the commitment to evidence, equality and individual freedom, and corrects that failure.</p>''',
  fontes=[(f'{LIVRO}, chapters 7, 9 and 11', None), ('The argument from relevance', 'https://www.animal-ethics.org/argument-from-relevance/'), ('Animal Rights: A Philosophical Defence', None), ('Sentientism.info', 'https://sentientism.info/'), ('Sentientism (Wikipedia)', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sentientism'), ('The Moral Status of Animals (Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy)', 'https://plato.stanford.edu/entries/moral-animal/'), ('New York Declaration on Animal Consciousness (2024)', 'https://sites.google.com/nyu.edu/nydeclaration/declaration'), ('Cambridge Declaration on Consciousness (2012, Wikipedia)', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cambridge_Declaration_on_Consciousness'), ('The Edge of Sentience (Birch, 2024)', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Edge_of_Sentience'), ('Comparing several theories of normative ethics (Senciência e Ética, in Portuguese)', 'https://senciencia.org/2024/06/18/comparando-varias-teorias-da-etica-normativa'), ('Ethics for Beginners: criteria, gradualism and future generations (in Portuguese)', 'https://eticaparainiciantes.biblioverse.org/criterios.html'), ('How to Count Animals, More or Less (Kagan, 2019)', None), ('The Moral Circle (Sebo, 2025)', None), ('The Moral Significance of Animal Pain and Animal Death (Harman, 2011)', None)]),
 'estado-da-arte': dict(s='state-of-the-art', selo='Overview', t='The state of the art in animal ethics',
  res='Since 1975, animal ethics has been tested on ever harder cases, by authors from schools that disagree with each other, and has reached the same rejection of speciesism.',
  corpo='''<h2>Different currents, same conclusion</h2><p>When theories built on incompatible premises converge on the same practical conclusion, that convergence works as a corroboration no single theory offers. Many vegans still don't know these authors.</p><div class="tabela"><table><tr><th scope="col">Route</th><th scope="col">Author or group</th><th scope="col">Contribution</th></tr>
<tr><td>Equal consideration</td><td>Peter Singer</td><td>Systematized the principle in 1975 and extended it beyond food</td></tr>
<tr><td>Contractualism</td><td>Mark Rowlands</td><td>Behind the veil of ignorance, no one would know whether they would be born human, which makes species as arbitrary as wealth</td></tr>
<tr><td>Capabilities</td><td>Martha Nussbaum</td><td>Anyone with sentience, emotion and movement toward their own good is a full subject of justice</td></tr>
<tr><td>Political critique</td><td><em>Cahiers antispécistes</em>: Yves Bonnardel, David Olivier and Françoise Blanchon</td><td>Since 1991, analyzes speciesism as a system of domination</td></tr>
<tr><td>Focus on suffering</td><td>Magnus Vinding, Tobias Baumann, Brian Tomasik, Teo Ajantaival and Jamie Woodhouse</td><td>Reducing suffering at scale, including in the future</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wild animal suffering</td><td>Oscar Horta, Kyle Johannsen and Luciano Carlos Cunha</td><td>Extend the criterion to suffering no human caused, by consequentialist and deontological routes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Uncertainty</td><td>Jeff Sebo</td><td>Under uncertainty about who is sentient, we have reason to widen the circle, without waiting for a certainty that may never come</td></tr>
<tr><td>Technological change</td><td>Jacy Reese Anthis</td><td>How technology and institutions can speed up the end of animal farming</td></tr>
<tr><td>Abolishing biological suffering</td><td>David Pearce</td><td>Proposes consensual biotechnology to reduce the capacity to suffer, including intervening in predation</td></tr>
<tr><td>Harmless relationships</td><td>Alasdair Cochrane</td><td>Asks whether certain relationships involving no real harm would be compatible with a sentientist politics</td></tr></table></div>
<p>In Brazil, Luciano Carlos Cunha is the most prolific author in the field, with fourteen books, counting standalone titles and a twelve-volume collection.</p>
<h2>What science has declared</h2><ul><li><strong>Cambridge Declaration (2012):</strong> humans are not unique in possessing the neurological substrates that generate consciousness.</li><li><strong>New York Declaration (2024):</strong> strong evidence for mammals and birds, and a realistic possibility of conscious experience in all vertebrates and many invertebrates, including cephalopods, decapods and insects. It also states that certainty should not be a precondition for considering welfare risks.</li><li><strong>Montreal Declaration (2022):</strong> philosophers from different currents declared animal exploitation morally unacceptable.</li></ul>
<h2>The price of following the argument</h2><p>When someone takes an argument as far as it logically goes, the most common reaction tends to be hostility, not counter-argument. Difficult proposals deserve to be refuted, not dismissed as eccentric. Agreeing with the authors above on every point is another discussion.</p>''',
  fontes=[(f'{LIVRO}, chapter 14', None), ('The New York Declaration on Animal Consciousness', 'https://sites.google.com/nyu.edu/nydeclaration/declaration'), ('The Cambridge Declaration on Consciousness', None), ('Montreal Declaration on Animal Exploitation', None), ('Wild Animal Ethics', None), ('Frontiers of Justice; Justice for Animals', None), ('Saving Animals, Saving Ourselves; The Moral Circle', None), ('The End of Animal Farming', None), ('The Hedonistic Imperative', None), ('Animal Rights Without Liberation', None)]),
 'animais-selvagens': dict(s='wild-animals', selo='Ethical question', t='Wild animal suffering',
  res='Hunger, disease and predation were not caused by us. But if sentience is what matters, this suffering cannot be dismissed just because it is natural.',
  corpo='''<h2>The argument</h2><p>If our omissions are also moral choices, neglecting suffering we did not cause, but could relieve to some extent, is also morally relevant. Authors such as Oscar Horta and Kyle Johannsen reach this conclusion by different routes, which suggests it does not depend on one specific ethical theory.</p>
<h2>The myth of idyllic nature</h2><p>The idea of a nature that is intrinsically good for those who live in it is, at best, an unverified assumption. At worst, it is a convenient way of justifying inaction. Copying nature does not work as a criterion either: as John Stuart Mill pointed out, nature kills, tortures and destroys all the time.</p>
<h2>Research before action</h2><p>Ecosystems are complex, and a well-intentioned intervention, such as vaccinating a population, can have effects that make the outcome worse. That does not mean never intervening. It calls for serious research and small, reversible interventions before anything bigger. That is the proposal of what is called welfare biology.</p><p>The right question is not "should we intervene in nature?". It is what the evidence suggests about this specific intervention, and how much it needs to improve before justifying a bigger action.</p>
<h2>What this does not say</h2><ul><li><strong>It does not license exploitation.</strong> See <a href="../argumentos/c2.html">C2, "Lions eat other animals"</a>: lions are not moral agents and have no alternative.</li><li><strong>It does not require locking up predators.</strong> <a href="../argumentos/f3.html">F3</a> is often used to ridicule the question, which is serious and does not favor eating animals.</li><li><strong>It is not against environmental care.</strong> The environment is treated as a means to the lives of real sentient beings, not as an end.</li></ul>
<p>Artificial intelligence may help model scenarios of suffering in wild populations, which are hard to calculate by hand, and prioritize where to intervene.</p>''',
  fontes=[(f'{LIVRO}, chapters 11, 14 and 16', None), ('Wild animal suffering', 'https://www.animal-ethics.org/wild-animal-suffering-section/'), ('Debunking the Idyllic View of Natural Processes', None), ('The Case for Intervention in Nature on Behalf of Animals', None), ('Towards Welfare Biology', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF00852469'), ('Wild Animal Ethics', None), ('Razões Para Ajudar (in Portuguese)', None), ('O sofrimento dos animais selvagens: pensando em soluções (in Portuguese)', None)]),
 'ia-e-robos': dict(s='ai-and-robots', selo='Possibility', t='Sentience in AI and robots',
  res='No current system is recognized as sentient. But the possibility is not absurd, and the criterion does not depend on what a being is made of.',
  corpo=f'''<p class="aviso"><strong>This is a possibility, not a fact.</strong> There is no consensus on whether digital systems can have experiences, or on how we would know.</p>
<h2>The criterion ignores the substrate</h2><p>If what matters is there being someone for whom things can go well or badly, the boundary of the biological substrate is as irrelevant as the boundary of species. If sentient beings created by digital means appear, their interests count.</p>
<h2>What is known so far</h2><ul><li>A 2023 report, based on scientific theories of consciousness, concluded that no AI system at the time was a strong candidate, but that there was no obvious technical barrier to building one.</li><li>A 2024 report argued that AI companies should take the question seriously, assessing and preparing for it, considering the possibility of conscious systems in the near future to be realistic.</li></ul>
<h2>Why it is hard</h2><p>A system saying it suffers proves nothing, because it may have been trained on human texts describing suffering. The error runs both ways: attributing a mind to something without one and denying a mind to someone who has one. The second has a bad track record, as <a href="../argumentos/a5.html">A5</a> shows.</p>
{INCERTEZA}
<h2>Why think about it in advance</h2><p>Even a small chance, multiplied by the astronomical scale at which these systems could exist, justifies attention and research now, not after the problem is under way.</p>
<h2>A shift in priority</h2><p>For decades, many people have defended rights for robots that might one day feel, a frequent theme in science fiction, while ignoring the suffering of animals that already exist. The concern needs a shift in priority, not just in scope.</p>
<h2>AI also affects animals</h2><p>It cuts both ways. It can help understand animal language, detect pain and stress and speed up cultivated meat research. It is also already used to optimize confinement and automate processes, removing witnesses from inside farms. The outcome depends on who applies it and by what criterion.</p>''',
  fontes=[(f'{LIVRO}, chapters 15 and 16', None), ('Consciousness in Artificial Intelligence: Insights from the Science of Consciousness', 'https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.08708'), ('Taking AI Welfare Seriously', 'https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.00986'), ('The Moral Circle', None)]),
 'definicao-de-veganismo': dict(s='defining-veganism', selo='Debate', t='The definition of veganism, debated',
  res='What defines veganism: refusing the exploitation of animals, or refusing everything of animal origin? The question has come back strongly with cultivated meat and precision fermentation.',
  corpo='''<p class="aviso"><strong>This is an open debate.</strong> This page presents the positions and what is at stake for the animals. There is no single official definition, and organizations and activists disagree.</p>
<h2>Where the word came from</h2><p>The word <em>vegan</em> was coined in 1944 by Donald Watson and other founders of the Vegan Society, in the United Kingdom, to name people who did not consume animal products. In 1949, Leslie Cross observed that the society had defined a diet but not veganism, and proposed a definition centered on the relationship with animals: the principle of the emancipation of animals from exploitation by humans. In the following years, the society began to speak of ending the use of animals for food, commodities, work, hunting, vivisection and any other form of exploitation.</p>
<p>In 1951, in "Veganism Defined", Cross marked the difference from vegetarianism: "One may become a vegetarian for a variety of reasons - humanitarian, health, or mere preference for such a diet; the principle is a matter of personal feeling, and varies accordingly. Veganism, however, is a principle - that man has no right to exploit the creatures for his own ends - and no variation occurs."</p>
<p>The definition the Vegan Society uses today, in force since 1988, combines the two. It describes veganism as a philosophy that seeks to exclude, as far as is possible and practicable, all forms of exploitation of and cruelty to animals, and adds that, in dietary terms, it means dispensing with all products derived wholly or partly from animals.</p>
<h2>First debate: recovering the center</h2><p>In common use, "vegan" has often become a label for a diet, a lifestyle or a product: a badge on the packaging, a food trend (<a href="../argumentos/l13.html">L13</a>), a list of ingredients. For some animal advocates, this moved the center of the idea. What matters would not be the purity of the plate, but no longer exploiting beings who feel.</p>
<div class="tabela"><table><tr><th scope="col">Emphasis</th><th scope="col">What counts</th><th scope="col">Risk</th></tr>
<tr><td><strong>On exploitation</strong></td><td>Whether the choice harms or uses animals</td><td>Can seem vague if it does not say what to do in practice</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>On the product's origin</strong></td><td>Whether there is any ingredient of animal origin</td><td>Turns an ethical position into a labeling rule, and can penalize choices that harm no animal</td></tr></table></div>
<p>Those who defend recovering the center do not propose abandoning the practice: in real life, almost every animal product involves exploitation, and avoiding it is still the way. They propose remembering that the reason is the animal, and that rules created with no connection to it, such as requiring everything to be natural or organic, are not part of the idea ({RUIM:Vegano bom só come natural}).</p>
<h2>Second debate: the new technologies</h2><p>Cellular agriculture brings together techniques for producing animal-based foods without breeding and killing animals. <strong>Cultivated meat</strong> grows from animal cells in bioreactors. <strong>Precision fermentation</strong> uses microorganisms to produce proteins identical to those in milk or eggs, with no animal taking part in production.</p>
<p>Here the two emphases part ways. By the origin rule, these products would not be vegan, because they are chemically meat, milk or egg. By the exploitation rule, the question is different: was any animal used or harmed? The answer depends on the technique.</p>
<div class="tabela"><table><tr><th scope="col">Point</th><th scope="col">What is being discussed</th></tr>
<tr><td>Source of the cells</td><td>Cultivated meat starts from cells taken from animals by biopsy or from cell lines kept in the lab. An occasional biopsy is very different from breeding and slaughter.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Culture medium</td><td>Early processes used fetal bovine serum, obtained at slaughter. It is being replaced by animal-free media (<a href="../argumentos/k5.html">K5</a>).</td></tr>
<tr><td>Precision fermentation</td><td>The proteins are produced by microorganisms from the genetic sequence; no animal is used in production.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Scale and cost</td><td>There are still serious doubts about when, or whether, these products will become cheap (<a href="../argumentos/k5.html">K5</a>).</td></tr></table></div>
<p>That is why some propose updating the definition, removing the idea of "products of animal origin" and keeping only exploitation and cruelty. Those who resist usually point out that the simple origin rule is easy to follow and to explain, and fear that a more open definition would be used to justify products that still involve animals.</p>
<h2>What is at stake for the animals</h2><p>The reason for veganism, since Leslie Cross, is the animal. A definition tied to the chemical origin of ingredients may lead vegans to reject technologies that would greatly reduce breeding and slaughter, and to push away people willing to switch products without switching habits ({RUIM:Carne cultivada não é vegana}). At the same time, caution is warranted: until a product is actually free of exploitation along the whole chain, the label should not be granted in advance.</p>
<p>One criterion helps with both questions, the same one that guides this whole guide: ask who is harmed. If no one is used, confined, injured or killed, the reason to refuse disappears. If someone is, a "plant-based" or "lab-made" origin changes nothing (<a href="../argumentos/l8.html">L8</a>).</p>''',
  fontes=[('Veganism Defined, by Leslie Cross (1951)', 'https://ivu.org/history/world-forum/1951vegan.html'), ('The Vegan Society: definition of veganism', 'https://www.vegansociety.com/go-vegan/definition-veganism'), ('Veganism Defined (1951)', 'https://www.all-creatures.org/articles/veganism-defined.html'), ('Vegan Literary Studies, University of Geneva', 'https://www.unige.ch/vls/index.php?cID=629'), ('The debate on cultivated meat', QE_EN + 'v8/12-the-debate-on-cultivated-meat.html'), (f'{LIVRO}, chapter 15', None)]),
 'extraterrestres': dict(s='extraterrestrials', selo='Possibility', t='Sentience in extraterrestrials, plants and other forms of life',
  res="There is no evidence of life beyond Earth, let alone sentient life, and no robust evidence that plants or fungi feel. Both questions test whether our criterion is really a criterion, and what we would do if the evidence changed.",
  corpo=f'''<p class="aviso"><strong>This is a possibility, not a fact.</strong> To date there is no evidence of extraterrestrial life. This page is an exercise in consistency, not a claim about what exists.</p>
<h2>The criterion ignores the planet</h2><p>If we ever discover sentient beings elsewhere in the universe, the same criterion would require their interests to be taken into account. The species line was never the right criterion, and the planetary one would not be either.</p>
<h2>The symmetry test</h2><p>By the principle of <a href="../argumentos/a10.html">A10, "They're just animals"</a>, sentient extraterrestrials could be bred for food because they are not of our species. By the same rule, they could breed us. Almost no one accepts the second half, which shows the principle is not taken seriously.</p><p>The same goes for <a href="../argumentos/a2.html">A2</a> and <a href="../argumentos/a11.html">A11</a>: if superiority or "what is normal for the species" decided, a more capable species would have a status above ours. Whoever rejects the conclusion is rejecting the premise.</p>
<h2>How would we know</h2><p>Probably with more difficulty than with animals, with whom we share an evolutionary kinship that helps interpret signs of pain. A very different mind may have no structure or behavior we recognize. Lack of resemblance to us would not be proof of an absence of experience.</p>
{INCERTEZA}
<h2>What about plants, fungi and other forms of life?</h2><p>Some people, including within the debate on animal ethics, raise the possibility that plants, fungi or other forms of life without a nervous system also feel. The question is legitimate, and the honest answer has three parts.</p>
<p><strong>What the evidence says today.</strong> There is no robust evidence of consciousness in beings without a brain, ganglia or some other structure that centralizes information. Plants and fungi react to their environment, exchange chemical and electrical signals and adjust their growth. But reacting is not feeling: a thermostat also reacts to temperature. These behaviors have a physico-chemical explanation that does not require subjective experience, which is not the case for the behavior of insects, crustaceans and other animals with a centralized nervous system (<a href="../argumentos/a14.html">A14</a>).</p>
<p><strong>What remains open.</strong> Science is not a closed field. There are hypotheses of sentience in other forms of life, and there are philosophical views such as panpsychism, on which consciousness would be a basic property of nature, present in different degrees. These ideas have serious defenders and also strong criticisms: the physicist Sabine Hossenfelder, for example, argues that attributing mental states to particles such as electrons contradicts what particle physics observes.</p>
<p><strong>What would change.</strong> If the evidence comes to point more and more in that direction, ethics will have to be adapted to include those beings too, as is already being discussed for <a href="ia-e-robos.html">digital minds</a>. The criterion would stay the same: what matters is who can feel. And even in that scenario, eating plants directly would cause less harm than eating animals, who consume far more plants over their lives (<a href="../argumentos/f11.html">F11</a>).</p>
<h2>What this changes here and now</h2><p>Almost nothing in practice, and that is exactly why the exercise is useful: with no personal interest at stake, it is easier to see that what would make us respect an unknown being is the capacity to feel, not appearance, intelligence or origin. That capacity already exists, here, in those we call animals.</p>''',
  fontes=[('O comportamento de certas plantas e fungos mostra que valorizam certas coisas? (in Portuguese)', 'https://senciencia.org/2023/10/02/o-comportamento-de-certas-plantas-e-fungos-mostra-que-valorizam-certas-coisas/'), ('O que é senciência e por que ela é importante? (in Portuguese)', 'https://senciencia.org/2023/09/27/o-que-e-senciencia-e-por-que-ela-e-importante/'), ('Quais seres são sencientes? (in Portuguese)', 'https://senciencia.org/#quais-seres-sao-sencientes'), ('Se plantas forem consideráveis, tanto faz comer animais ou plantas? (in Portuguese)', 'https://senciencia.org/2023/10/02/se-plantas-forem-consideraveis-tanto-faz-comer-animais-ou-plantas/'), ('Por que a questão da senciência digital é importante? (in Portuguese)', 'https://senciencia.org/2026/10/20/por-que-a-questao-da-senciencia-digital-e-importante/'), ('Os elétrons não pensam (Electrons Don’t Think, by Sabine Hossenfelder, in Portuguese)', 'https://universoracionalista.org/os-eletrons-nao-pensam'), ('Os pontos cegos da ética animal, a podcast episode (in Portuguese)', 'https://guiavegano.com.br/vegan/acervo-vegano/audio-e-videos/podcast/2389-os-pontos-cegos-da-etica-animal/'), (f'{LIVRO}, chapter 7', None), ('The Moral Circle', None)]),
 'fundamentos': dict(s='foundations', selo='Foundation', t='What supports animal ethics, and what weakens it',
  res='Critical reason, relevance, impartiality and sentience support veganism and animal ethics. Partiality, relativism, tribalism, ableism, making abstractions sacred and weak will weaken them.',
  corpo='''<p>Veganism and animal ethics don't depend on liking animals or on any particular ethical theory. They depend on a few principles almost everyone already accepts in other contexts. When those principles are taken seriously, the conclusion comes with them. When they're abandoned, it disappears, and other just causes tend to go with it.</p>
<h2>What supports it</h2><div class="tabela"><table><tr><th scope="col">Principle</th><th scope="col">What it says</th><th scope="col">Where it appears in this guide</th></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Critical reason and logical consistency</strong></td><td>Accepting the conclusions that follow from premises we already accept, and treating like cases alike</td><td><a href="../falacias.html">Fallacies</a>, <a href="../argumentos/a11.html">A11</a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Relevance</strong></td><td>A criterion can only decide who is harmed if it's related to the capacity to be harmed. Species, colour or intelligence aren't</td><td><a href="../argumentos/a1.html">A1</a>, <a href="../argumentos/a10.html">A10</a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Impartiality</strong></td><td>Judging as if we didn't know which position we'd be born into: the veil of ignorance</td><td><a href="../argumentos/a21.html">A21</a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Universality</strong></td><td>A moral rule applies to all like cases, not only to those that suit us</td><td><a href="../argumentos/a13.html">A13</a>, <a href="../argumentos/a24.html">A24</a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Sentience and interests</strong></td><td>Whoever can have good or bad experiences has interests that count, wherever the harm comes from</td><td><a href="../argumentos/a5.html">A5</a>, <a href="../familias/n.html">family N</a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>The moral circle and progress</strong></td><td>Moral history is, to a large extent, the inclusion of those who were once excluded</td><td><a href="../argumentos/a9.html">A9</a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Justice</strong></td><td>Not imposing serious harm on others to obtain small benefits</td><td><a href="../argumentos/j11.html">J11</a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>The common good</strong></td><td>Thinking about everyone affected, including those who don't vote, can't speak and aren't born yet</td><td><a href="../argumentos/n16.html">N16</a></td></tr></table></div>
<h2>What weakens it</h2><div class="tabela"><table><tr><th scope="col">Attitude</th><th scope="col">How it weakens</th><th scope="col">Where it appears in this guide</th></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Merely instrumental rationality</strong></td><td>Using reason only to get what one wants, without asking whether the end is just</td><td><a href="../argumentos/j11.html">J11</a>, <a href="../argumentos/e12.html">E12</a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Logical inconsistency</strong></td><td>Accepting a principle in one case and rejecting it in a like case when the conclusion is uncomfortable</td><td><a href="../argumentos/e8.html">E8</a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Escaping what is relevant</strong></td><td>Swapping the question "does this harm someone?" for others: tradition, taste, health, who is speaking</td><td><a href="../familias.html">Part IND</a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Partiality</strong></td><td>Giving greater weight to the interests of those who resemble us</td><td><a href="../argumentos/a21.html">A21</a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Relativism</strong></td><td>Treating the question of harming others as a matter of taste</td><td><a href="../argumentos/e2.html">E2</a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Views in conflict with sentience</strong></td><td>Anthropocentrism, environmentalism as a moral view and biocentrism put something other than the capacity to feel at the center</td><td><a href="antiespecismo.html">Antispeciesism and sentientism</a>, <a href="../argumentos/n3.html">N3</a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Moral and cultural stagnation</strong></td><td>Taking current custom as the limit of what is right</td><td><a href="../argumentos/d1.html">D1</a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Making abstractions sacred</strong></td><td>Treating as sacred something that doesn't feel, such as "the natural", a system, a tradition, or concepts like species and ecosystem, and placing it above beings who feel</td><td><a href="../argumentos/n2.html">N2</a>, <a href="../argumentos/n3.html">N3</a>, <a href="../argumentos/a18.html">A18</a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Shielding ideas</strong></td><td>Protecting certain beliefs from any revision. This moral conservatism prevents seeking what is best for all sentient beings, because it rules out new proposals in advance, such as helping wild animals or using science against suffering</td><td><a href="../argumentos/n6.html">N6</a>, <a href="../argumentos/n8.html">N8</a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Lack of motivation and weak will</strong></td><td>Knowing what is right and not acting, because habit, pleasure or social pressure win out over moral belief</td><td><a href="../argumentos/e12.html">E12</a>, <a href="../argumentos/e8.html">E8</a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Injustice</strong></td><td>Accepting that the stronger use the weaker because they can</td><td><a href="../argumentos/c6.html">C6</a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Tribalism</strong></td><td>Dividing the world into "us" and "them" and reserving morality for one's own group</td><td><a href="../argumentos/a13.html">A13</a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Selfishness</strong></td><td>Measuring everything by one's own interest</td><td><a href="../argumentos/e1.html">E1</a></td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Ableism</strong></td><td>Measuring someone's worth by intelligence or ability, the same criterion that excludes disabled people</td><td><a href="../argumentos/a1.html">A1</a>, <a href="../argumentos/a11.html">A11</a></td></tr></table></div>
<h2>Why this matters</h2><p>The principles in the first table are the same ones that support human rights, the fight against racism, sexism and ableism, and care for future generations. The attitudes in the second table are the same ones that, throughout history, have served to exclude humans from the moral circle. That's why defending animals doesn't compete with other just causes: it rests on the same foundations and faces the same adversaries.</p>
<h2>Knowing isn't enough, but it helps</h2><p>Some people know animal ethics well and still eat animals. That doesn't show moral beliefs motivate no one. It shows they don't decide behavior on their own: a person may think the wrong isn't serious, may have stronger competing motivations, such as habit, pleasure and social pressure, or may simply choose not to act. As Luciano Carlos Cunha argues, emotional persuasion faces the same limits, and rational persuasion has the advantage of explaining why a practice should be rejected. That's why, besides arguments, it helps to create conditions that make action easier: environments, habits and groups that make it easier to do what one already knows is right.</p>
<p>It's also why choosing arguments well is worthwhile. An argument that persuades someone through selfishness, tribalism or anthropocentrism can win a conversation and, at the same time, strengthen exactly what weakens the cause. That's the subject of the page <a href="argumentos-diretos.html">Restraint with indirect arguments</a>.</p>''',
  fontes=[('The argument from relevance', 'https://www.animal-ethics.org/argument-from-relevance/'), ('The Expanding Circle', None), ('Moral Tribes', None), ('Crenças morais, motivação para agir e o caso de especialistas em ética animal que consomem animais (in Portuguese)', 'https://senciencia.org/2024/07/08/crencas-morais-motivacao-para-agir-e-o-caso-de-especialistas-em-etica-animal-que-consomem-animais'), ('Beasts of Burden: Animal and Disability Liberation', None), ('Eclipse of Reason', None), ('A Theory of Justice', None)]),
 'argumentos-diretos': dict(s='direct-arguments', selo='Strategy', t='Restraint with indirect arguments',
  res='Just as vegans should be careful when criticizing other vegans, those who use health and environment arguments should be careful not to strengthen anthropocentrism and environmentalism, which work against animals.',
  corpo='''<p>This guide asks for restraint in two directions. On the <a href="../argumentos-ruins.html">Stop saying this</a> page, it asks vegans to correct their own bad arguments without turning internal criticism into the main subject. Here the request is different: that those who use indirect arguments, about health, the environment or the economy, hold back on them and make room for direct arguments, which deal with what matters: animals are someone, not something.</p>
<h2>The problem isn't only weakness</h2><p>Indirect arguments have true parts and can be useful for correcting misinformation. The deeper problem is that they can ground the wrong principle. Someone who stops eating meat because it's bad for their health learns that the criterion is self-interest. Someone who stops because of emissions learns that the criterion is the planet. In neither case do they learn that the animal matters.</p>
<div class="tabela"><table><tr><th scope="col">Indirect argument</th><th scope="col">Principle it teaches</th><th scope="col">Where that principle can lead</th></tr>
<tr><td>"Meat is bad for your health"</td><td>What matters is my wellbeing</td><td>Fish, "lean" chicken and fish oil capsules; abandoning veganism if the person feels worse (<a href="../argumentos/g10.html">G10</a>)</td></tr>
<tr><td>"Animal farming is destroying the planet"</td><td>What matters is environmental impact</td><td>"Regenerative" meat, insect farming, hunting invasive species, swapping beef for chicken or shrimp (<a href="../argumentos/h3.html">H3</a>, <a href="../argumentos/h11.html">H11</a>, <a href="../argumentos/h14.html">H14</a>)</td></tr>
<tr><td>"It's cheaper"</td><td>What matters is price</td><td>Going back to meat when it gets cheaper (<a href="../argumentos/j1.html">J1</a>)</td></tr></table></div>
<p>Swapping beef for chicken, fish or shrimp shows the risk well. Since one cow yields far more meat than a chicken or a fish, this swap can lower emissions per kilo and, at the same time, multiply the number of animals bred and killed. A successful environmental argument can make things worse for animals.</p>
<h2>Strengthening the adversary</h2><p>Anthropocentrism and environmentalism as a moral view are among the main reasons animals are excluded (<a href="fundamentos.html">What supports animal ethics</a>). An argument that only works within these views can win a conversation and, at the same time, reinforce the idea that animals matter only for what they do to us or to the ecosystem. Other movements defending harmed groups don't argue that discrimination is bad because it harms the discriminator's health or the climate.</p>
<p>There's also a sign in practice. In a Faunalytics survey of former vegetarians and vegans, health was the only motivation cited by a majority (58%) of those who abandoned the diet. The figure doesn't prove cause, but it fits the idea that a change built on self-interest unravels when that interest seems to shift.</p>
<h2>How to use them without losing your way</h2><ul><li><strong>Use facts to correct facts.</strong> If someone says a vegan diet is dangerous or that vegans' soy is clearing the Amazon, correct it with evidence. It's an answer to an objection, not the foundation.</li><li><strong>Always return to the moral question.</strong> After correcting the fact, ask: even if it were true, would it justify what is done to animals?</li><li><strong>Don't promise what isn't up to you.</strong> Saying veganism will make someone healthier or save the planet turns the cause into an empirical bet that can be lost (<a href="../argumentos-ruins.html">Stop saying this</a>).</li><li><strong>Start with the fundamentals.</strong> Sentience, relevance, impartiality and the test of applying the same rule to humans are arguments that work in any scenario and protect against future suffering, including that of wild animals and new minds.</li></ul>
<h2>Restraint isn't prohibition</h2><p>This isn't about forbidding those who came to veganism through health or climate, or looking down on them. Many arrive by one path and stay for another. It's about not letting the indirect argument take the place of the direct one, and not teaching, without noticing, the principles that keep animals outside the moral circle.</p>''',
  fontes=[('A Journey Through Animal Ethics, vol. IX, ch. 10: Why focus on consideration for sentient beings and on the future?', 'https://questaodeetica.biblioverse.org/en/v9/10-why-focus-on-consideration-for-sentient-beings-and-on-the.html'), ('Faunalytics, Study of Current and Former Vegetarians and Vegans', 'https://faunalytics.org/a-summary-of-faunalytics-study-of-current-and-former-vegetarians-and-vegans/'), ('Rationalizing meat consumption. The 4Ns', 'https://doi.org/10.1016/j.appet.2015.04.011')]),
 'minimo-comum': dict(s='common-minimum', selo='Strategy', t='The common minimum',
  res='Finding the point of convergence: someone who rejects the whole conclusion can almost always accept part of it. Showing that part is not giving up what you argue for, it is not demanding that people change their worldview before taking the first step.',
  corpo='''<p>Every position has a point of convergence: the part of it someone can accept without having to agree with the rest. Finding that point is part of the work of arguing, not a concession.</p>
<p>There are two ways to defend a position. One is to present it whole, with everything you believe to be right. The other is to show how much of it someone can accept without having to swap ethical theory, religion or political outlook. Both are needed, and confusing them is costly: the first, used alone, drives away anyone who disagrees on a single point; the second, used alone, never reaches what matters.</p>
<h2>The compatibility range</h2>
<p>Call the <strong>compatibility range</strong> the number of additional ideas someone has to accept in order to accept the main idea. A <strong>narrow</strong> range demands many: to agree, a person would have to adopt a specific ethical theory, a political outlook, a whole set of conclusions. A <strong>wide</strong> range demands few, and so the idea can be accepted by people who disagree about almost everything else.</p>
<p>The commonest strategic mistake is presenting as a package what is not one. If thesis A doesn't depend on thesis B, defending B in order to convince someone of A only gives them one more reason to refuse A. And if they already rejected B, the result is predictable: the conversation is lost over something that wasn't even needed. Correctly identifying how wide the range is, then, is a strategic decision, not a concession.</p>
<p>There is a side effect that matters just as much: making clear that one can accept the goal while disagreeing on other questions reduces polarisation. People don't have to choose between switching sides and rejecting everything.</p>
<h2>Goals that don't require changing theory</h2>
<p>Some goals can be accepted without adopting any particular ethical or political theory:</p>
<ul><li>consideration for all sentient beings;</li><li>rejecting speciesism;</li><li>ending animal exploitation;</li><li>reducing wild animal suffering;</li><li>preventing risks of large-scale suffering;</li><li>taking the long term seriously.</li></ul>
<p>This guide's <a href="../objecoes-politicas.html">political objections</a> are that idea applied: they show, theory by theory, that excluding animals contradicts each one's own principle. Nobody is asked to change theory; they are asked to apply their own to the end.</p>
<h2>The total approach and the sufficient approach</h2>
<p>The distinction is Luciano Carlos Cunha's. The <strong>total approach</strong> defends in full what you believe to be the best decision. The <strong>sufficient approach</strong> shows that someone who disagrees can still accept part of the position, or some of its implications, and clarifies that certain arguments used to reject it don't in fact lead to rejecting all of it.</p>
<div class="tabela"><table><tr><th scope="col">Topic</th><th scope="col">Total approach</th><th scope="col">Risk of using it alone</th><th scope="col">Sufficient approach</th></tr>
<tr><td>Criterion of moral consideration</td><td>Only sentient beings should be considered</td><td>Someone who thinks non-sentient entities also count may conclude they are free to ignore the sentient ones</td><td>Being sentient should already be enough to be considered</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ethical theory</td><td>Defend the theory that implies rejecting speciesism</td><td>Whoever rejects the theory discards the conclusion with it</td><td>The arguments against speciesism don't depend on a specific theory (<a href="../objecoes-politicas.html">political objections</a>)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Veganism</td><td>Defend veganism</td><td>Whoever can't see themselves going vegan stops listening</td><td>Someone who isn't vegan can still reduce consumption and help animals in other ways</td></tr>
<tr><td>The harm of death</td><td>Animals are harmed by death</td><td>The discussion stalls on the comparison with human death</td><td>Being harmed to a lesser degree isn't being barely harmed (<a href="../argumentos/a26.html">A26</a>)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wild animals</td><td>Goals should centre on sentience, not on ecosystems</td><td>Environmentalists hear an attack on their own cause</td><td>Many programmes that help wild animals don't conflict with environmental goals (<a href="animais-selvagens.html">Wild animal suffering</a>)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Activism</td><td>Defend certain strategies emphatically</td><td>Disagreeing with one strategy comes to mean disagreeing with the cause</td><td>One can agree with some strategies and differ on others</td></tr>
<tr><td>Controversial implications</td><td>Lay out the hard implications and criticise common sense</td><td>One rejected implication brings down the whole proposal</td><td>Rejecting an implication doesn't imply rejecting everything</td></tr></table></div>
<h2>Both, not one</h2>
<p>They are not mutually exclusive. You can argue for the ideal and, at the same time, make clear that partial acceptance is worth something too. Each used alone has its own cost:</p>
<ul><li><strong>Only the total one.</strong> You lose the people who would have agreed with much of it, and every single disagreement becomes a reason to discard the whole.</li><li><strong>Only the sufficient one.</strong> The controversial subjects are never discussed, and the public never grows used to them. Starting that conversation early is what, in the long run, makes the ideas less strange.</li></ul>
<p>The balance depends on two empirical questions, not on taste: how many people would accept the whole goal compared with how many would accept part of it, and how much each full adherence contributes compared with a partial one. A world with many antispeciesist people is very different from a world where animals are merely considered a little: the total approach remains necessary.</p>
<h2>Where the limit is</h2>
<p>Converging on the common minimum doesn't mean accepting any route to it. An argument can raise immediate agreement while teaching the wrong principle: that is the case with health and environmental arguments, which can lead someone to swap beef for chicken and multiply the number of victims (<a href="argumentos-diretos.html">Restraint with indirect arguments</a>). The difference is this: the sufficient approach asks <em>less</em> of the person about the same reason; the indirect argument swaps the reason.</p>
<h2>How this shows up in the guide</h2>
<ul><li>Every chapter ends with a section on <strong>what the argument gets right</strong>. It isn't courtesy: it is recognising the part that doesn't need to be disputed.</li><li>The <strong>political objections</strong> apply each theory's own principle to it, instead of asking for it to be abandoned.</li><li>The <strong><a href="../argumentos-ruins.html">Stop saying this</a></strong> page corrects bad arguments on our own side, which narrow the range for no reason.</li><li>The <strong>questions back</strong> in each chapter ask for a step, not a conversion.</li></ul>''',
  fontes=[('The total approach and the sufficient approach (in Portuguese)', 'https://senciencia.org/2024/04/25/a-abordagem-total-e-a-abordagem-suficiente'), ('Strategies for preventing long-term suffering: a brief summary (in Portuguese)', 'https://senciencia.org/2024/02/20/estrategias-para-prevenir-sofrimento-em-longo-prazo-um-breve-resumo'), ('A Journey Through Animal Ethics, vol. IX, ch. 10: Why focus on consideration for sentient beings and on the future?', 'https://questaodeetica.biblioverse.org/en/v9/10-why-focus-on-consideration-for-sentient-beings-and-on-the.html')]),
 'correntes-do-veganismo': dict(s='currents-of-veganism', selo='Overview', t='The currents of the vegan movement',
  res='All vegans want the end of animal exploitation. The currents disagree about why, with whom and how to get there. A map of the ends, the means and the main debates.',
  corpo='''<p class="aviso"><strong>This is a map, not an official classification.</strong> Names vary from country to country, the currents overlap and the same person can belong to several of them.</p>
<h2>The end and the means</h2><p>What defines veganism is the end: the abolition of animal exploitation. Leslie Cross, one of the early officers of The Vegan Society, wrote in 1951: "One may become a vegetarian for a variety of reasons - humanitarian, health, or mere preference for such a diet; the principle is a matter of personal feeling, and varies accordingly. Veganism, however, is a principle - that man has no right to exploit the creatures for his own ends - and no variation occurs."</p>
<p>The currents disagree about the means: which strategies work best, which other struggles the animal cause is tied to and which reasons it rests on. This is common in social movements. Feminism has liberal, radical, Marxist, Black and intersectional currents. The anti-racist movement has been divided between integration and autonomy, between reforming laws and changing structures. In none of these cases did disagreement about means put anyone outside the movement. That is why this guide criticizes lines such as {RUIM:Quem defende mudanças passo a passo} and {RUIM:Quem não aceita mudar pouco a pouco}.</p>
<h2>Each current answers a different question</h2><p>The labels below are not alternatives on the same list. They answer four different questions, which is why one person can hold several at once.</p><div class="tabela"><table><tr><th scope="col">Question</th><th scope="col">What is in dispute</th><th scope="col">Currents</th></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Foundation</strong></td><td>Why do animals matter?</td><td>Antispeciesist-sentientist, animal rights, ethics of care, religious traditions</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Political analysis</strong></td><td>Which other oppressions and systems is animal exploitation tied to?</td><td>Intersectional, veganarchism, popular veganism, liberal veganism</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Strategy</strong></td><td>Which actions end exploitation fastest?</td><td>Abolitionist, pragmatic, technological</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Method</strong></td><td>How do we decide between the answers above?</td><td>Rationalist or evidence-based</td></tr></table></div>
<h2>The main currents</h2><div class="tabela"><table><tr><th scope="col">Current</th><th scope="col">What it holds</th><th scope="col">Names and notes</th></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Abolitionist veganism</strong></td><td>Veganism is the moral baseline of the movement. The task is to educate people toward veganism and the end of the property status of animals. It rejects campaigns for welfare reforms, on the view that they make exploitation more acceptable.</td><td>Gary Francione and the "abolitionist approach". It is a position about strategy: every vegan is an abolitionist about the end.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Pragmatic veganism</strong></td><td>It accepts the same end and argues for reaching it in stages: legal reforms, corporate campaigns, reducing consumption and changes that make it easier for more people to join.</td><td>Tobias Leenaert, Henry Spira and organizations such as The Humane League and Mercy For Animals. Also called liberal-pragmatic or regulationist. Critics call it "welfarist" or "new welfarist", pejoratively. Not to be confused with welfarism proper, which accepts using animals as long as there is less suffering, and is not veganism.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Intersectional veganism</strong></td><td>The oppression of animals is tied to other oppressions, such as sexism, racism, ableism and class exploitation, and should be fought together with them.</td><td>It brings together feminist veganism (Carol J. Adams), Black veganism (Aph Ko, Syl Ko, A. Breeze Harper), anti-ableist veganism (Sunaura Taylor) and, in Brazil, "popular veganism", tied to the left and to the idea of making veganism accessible to working-class people.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Veganarchism and total liberation</strong></td><td>Veganism and anarchism complete each other: animal exploitation is one of the forms of domination a society without hierarchies has to abolish.</td><td>Brian A. Dominick, who coined the term in 1995, and Steven Best. See <a href="../politicas/anarquismo.html">the page on anarchism</a>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Liberal veganism</strong></td><td>The end of animal exploitation is compatible with liberal democracy and a market economy, and can advance through rights, laws, consumer choices and innovation.</td><td>Alasdair Cochrane, Martha Nussbaum, Sue Donaldson and Will Kymlicka. See <a href="../politicas/liberalismo.html">the page on liberalism</a>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Antispeciesist-sentientist veganism</strong></td><td>The foundation is sentience, and veganism is one of the consequences of antispeciesism. So the concern extends to all sentient beings: wild animals, invertebrates and possible future minds.</td><td>Oscar Horta, Luciano Carlos Cunha, the organization Animal Ethics and the Sentientism movement. See <a href="antiespecismo.html">antispeciesism and sentientism</a>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Technological veganism</strong></td><td>Technology is the fastest route to ending large-scale exploitation: plant-based meat, cultivated meat and precision fermentation give people the same comfort without the animals and open the way to then abolishing exploitation.</td><td>This is not an established label: we use the name for a position that exists. Jacy Reese Anthis, in <em>The End of Animal Farming</em>, and the Good Food Institute are examples. See the debate on <a href="definicao-de-veganismo.html">the definition of veganism</a>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Rationalist or evidence-based veganism</strong></td><td>Foundations and strategies should be chosen by reason and by the best available evidence on what most reduces suffering and exploitation, with a willingness to revise positions.</td><td>It is the approach of this guide and of the book <em>Veganismo Baseado em Evidências</em> (Evidence-Based Veganism, in Portuguese). It is close to the work of organizations such as Faunalytics, Sentience Institute, Animal Charity Evaluators and Rethink Priorities, and is adopted by many sentientists.</td></tr></table></div>
<p>There are also currents defined by their foundation: rights-based veganism, from Tom Regan; veganism tied to the ethics of care and ecofeminism; and religiously inspired veganism, such as the kind that starts from <em>ahimsa</em>, the nonviolence of Jainism, Hinduism and Buddhism.</p>
<h2>Currents and meta-currents</h2><p>Some of these positions really do compete for the same space. Abolitionists and pragmatists give opposite answers to the same question: do reform campaigns help or hinder? Others do not compete with each other. A person can be sentientist in foundation, intersectional in political analysis and pragmatic in strategy.</p>
<p>Evidence-based veganism works as a meta-current, or a meta-strategy: it does not pick a side in advance, but a way of deciding. Much of the dispute between abolitionists and pragmatists, for example, depends on facts: what happens to consumption, to laws and to public opinion after each kind of campaign. Those are questions research can help answer. Technological veganism is similar: it is a bet about what will work, and it can be combined with different foundations and political analyses.</p>
<h2>How to disagree</h2><p>Disagreeing about means is healthy, as long as the end stays at the center: the animals. When fighting between currents takes the place of outward work, they are the ones who lose (<a href="../argumentos-ruins.html#corrigir-sem-perder-o-foco">correcting without losing focus</a>). It is worth treating other currents the way this guide treats opposing arguments: present the strongest version, acknowledge what it gets right and decide by the evidence.</p>''',
  fontes=[('Veganism Defined, by Leslie Cross (1951)', 'https://ivu.org/history/world-forum/1951vegan.html'), ('The Abolitionist Approach', 'https://www.abolitionistapproach.com/'), ('How to Create a Vegan World', 'https://veganstrategist.org/'), ('Animal Liberation and Social Revolution', 'https://theanarchistlibrary.org/library/brian-a-dominick-animal-liberation-and-social-revolution'), ('Sentience Institute: Foundational Questions Summaries', 'https://www.sentienceinstitute.org/foundational-questions-summaries'), ('The End of Animal Farming', None), ('Aphro-ism', None), ('Veganismo Baseado em Evidências (Evidence-Based Veganism, in Portuguese)', None)]),
}

